  "generated_text": "First, we solve the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 3x - 5 = 0\\) to find the roots \\(d\\) and \\(e\\).\n\nWe use the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\).\n\nThe roots \\(d\\) and \\(e\\) are given by:\n\\[ d = \\frac{-b + \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\]\n\\[ e = \\frac{-b - \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\((d-1)(e-1)\\).\n\nUsing the properties of roots, we have:\n\\[ (d-1)(e-1) = de - d - e + 1 \\]\n\nSubstitute the value of \\(de\\) from the quadratic equation:\n\\[ de = \\frac{-5}{2} \\]\n\nThus:\n\\[ (d-1)(e-1) = \\frac{-5}{2} - \\frac{3}{2} + 1 = -3 \\]\n\n\\boxed{-3}",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[-3, '-3']",
    "gold_answer": "By Vieta's formulas, $d + e = -\\frac{3}{2}$ and $de = -\\frac{5}{2},$ so\n\\[(d - 1)(e - 1) = de - (d + e) + 1 = -\\frac{5}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} + 1 = \\boxed{0}.\\]",
    "is_correct": false
  },
